## Introduction

In this guide, I walk through the process of using `git`, GitHub, and the command line interface (CLI) to manage a project with version control. I treat each step as a diary entry, explaining not just what I did, but **why** I did it and what it achieves. My goal is to show that I understand how these tools work together for reproducible and collaborative data science.

---

## Step 1: Setting Up the Project and Creating `example.qmd`

The first thing I did was create a new project in Positron via **File > New Project**. I chose to create it as a new directory called `assignment2`. This gave me a clean, self-contained folder to work in — which is important for reproducibility, because keeping all project files in one place means anyone can open the project and everything will work relative to that root folder.

Inside the project, I created this file, `example.qmd`. A `.qmd` file is a Quarto Markdown document — it lets me mix written text with R code chunks, and then render everything into a formatted HTML report. I rendered the file using the **Render** button (or by running `quarto render example.qmd` in the terminal), which produced `example.html`.

Here is a simple piece of R code I included to confirm the document renders correctly:

```{r}
summary(cars)
```

Seeing the rendered HTML output confirmed that my Quarto setup was working properly before I started doing anything with git.

---

## Step 2: Initialising Git and Pushing to GitHub

Once I had a working project, I wanted to put it under version control. Version control lets me track every change I make over time, so I can always go back to an earlier version if something breaks. I also wanted to push it to GitHub so it is backed up remotely and could be shared with collaborators.

I opened the terminal and ran the following commands one by one:

```bash
# Initialise this folder as a git repository
git init
```

`git init` creates a hidden `.git` folder inside my project directory. This folder is where git stores all the version history and configuration. Without this step, git has no idea this folder is a repository.

```bash
# Tell git who I am (only needed once per machine)
git config --global user.name "Your Name"
git config --global user.email "your@email.com"
```

I set my identity so that every commit is attributed to me. This matters in collaborative settings where multiple people are committing to the same repository.

```bash
# Stage all files in the project for the first commit
git add .
```

`git add .` stages everything in the current directory. Staging means I am telling git "these are the changes I want to include in the next commit." Think of it like putting items into a box before sealing it — I can choose exactly what goes in before I commit.

```bash
# Create the first commit
git commit -m "Initial commit: add project files and example.qmd"
```

`git commit` takes a snapshot of everything that is staged and saves it permanently to the history. The `-m` flag lets me write a short message describing what this commit contains. Clear commit messages are important — they help me (and others) understand what changed and why, without having to read through all the code.

```bash
# Link the local repository to a new GitHub repository
git remote add origin https://github.com/yourusername/assignment2.git

# Push the commit to GitHub
git push -u origin main
```

`git remote add origin` tells my local git where the remote repository lives — in this case, on GitHub. `git push -u origin main` uploads my commit to GitHub and sets `origin/main` as the default remote branch so that future `git push` commands on `main` know where to go automatically.

---

## Step 3: Creating `testbranch`, Making Changes, and Pushing

At this point, I wanted to experiment with changes to `example.qmd` without touching the stable version on `main`. This is exactly what **branches** are for — they let me work on something new in isolation, and only merge it back when I am happy with it.

```bash
# Create a new branch called testbranch and switch to it immediately
git checkout -b testbranch
```

`git checkout -b testbranch` does two things at once: it creates the branch and switches me to it. From this point on, any commits I make will go onto `testbranch`, not `main`. The `main` branch remains exactly as it was.

I then opened `example.qmd` and added a new section — a short paragraph introducing the cars dataset. After saving the file:

```bash
# Stage the modified file
git add example.qmd

# Commit the change with a descriptive message
git commit -m "Add dataset introduction section to example.qmd on testbranch"
```

```bash
# Push testbranch to GitHub and set up tracking
git push -u origin testbranch
```

I pushed `testbranch` to GitHub with `-u origin testbranch` so that this branch also exists on the remote. This means a collaborator could also see and work on `testbranch` from their own machine.

---

## Step 4: Adding the Data Folder and Amending the Commit

After committing, I realised I also needed to add a `data` folder containing the data from Assignment 1. Rather than creating a separate new commit just for the data (which would clutter the history), I decided to **amend** the previous commit to include it. Amending is like going back and fixing the last commit before anyone else has based work on it.

```bash
# Create the data folder and copy in the Assignment 1 data file
mkdir data
cp /path/to/your/assignment1data.csv data/
```

```bash
# Stage the new data folder
git add data/
```

```bash
# Amend the previous commit to include the data folder
# --no-edit means I keep the same commit message as before
git commit --amend --no-edit
```

`git commit --amend` rewrites the most recent commit to include whatever is newly staged. The commit hash changes as a result, because the content of the commit has changed. This is why I cannot just do a regular `git push` — the remote and local histories now differ.

```bash
# Force push to update the remote with the amended commit
git push --force origin testbranch
```

I used `git push --force` to overwrite the old commit on GitHub with the new amended one. I was careful to only use `--force` here because I am the only person working on `testbranch` — force pushing on a shared branch could erase other people's work, which is why it should always be used with caution.

---

## Step 5: Switching to `main` and Creating a Conflicting Change

Now I switched back to `main` to make a change that would deliberately conflict with what I did on `testbranch`. This is so I can practise identifying and resolving merge conflicts — a skill that is essential when collaborating with others.

```bash
# Switch back to the main branch
git checkout main
```

I modified the **same lines** in `example.qmd` that I had changed on `testbranch`, but wrote something different. For example, where `testbranch` had added "This dataset contains car speed data", I wrote "This section explores the cars dataset" on `main` instead. By editing the same section differently on both branches, I ensured git would not be able to automatically choose one version over the other.

```bash
# Stage the modified file
git add example.qmd

# Commit with a clear message
git commit -m "Modify example.qmd on main: update dataset description"

# Push to GitHub
git push origin main
```

This commit now lives on `main`, while a different version of the same lines exists on `testbranch`. The stage is set for a merge conflict.

---

## Step 6: Merging `testbranch` into `main` and Resolving the Conflict

With conflicting changes on both branches, I attempted to merge `testbranch` into `main`:

```bash
# Make sure I am on main
git checkout main

# Attempt the merge
git merge testbranch
```

Git immediately detected a conflict and printed:

```
Auto-merging example.qmd
CONFLICT (content): Merge conflict in example.qmd
Automatic merge failed; fix conflicts then commit the result.
```

When I opened `example.qmd`, I saw the conflict markers git had inserted:

```
<<<<<<< HEAD
This section explores the cars dataset
=======
This dataset contains car speed data
>>>>>>> testbranch
```

The section between `<<<<<<< HEAD` and `=======` is what is on `main`. The section between `=======` and `>>>>>>> testbranch` is what came from `testbranch`. Git cannot decide which version is correct, so it leaves the decision to me.

I resolved the conflict by combining both versions into one sentence that made sense:

```
This section explores the cars dataset, which contains car speed data.
```

I removed the conflict markers entirely, saved the file, and completed the merge:

```bash
# Stage the resolved file
git add example.qmd

# Complete the merge with a commit
git commit -m "Resolve merge conflict: combine dataset descriptions from main and testbranch"

# Push the resolved merge to GitHub
git push origin main
```

On GitHub, I could now see that both branches had been successfully merged, with the full commit history preserved.

---

## Step 7: Tagging the Commit as `v1.0`

Now that the merge was complete and the project was in a clean, stable state, I wanted to mark this point in history as version 1.0. Git **tags** let me attach a meaningful label to a specific commit so I (and collaborators) can find it easily later — for example, when a project reaches a milestone or a public release.

```bash
# Create an annotated tag on the current commit
git tag -a v1.0 -m "Version 1.0: project complete, merge conflict resolved"

# Push the tag to GitHub
git push origin v1.0
```

I used an **annotated tag** (`-a`) rather than a lightweight tag because annotated tags store extra information: the tagger's name, email, date, and a message. This makes them more informative for marking releases. Tags are not pushed automatically with a regular `git push`, so I had to push the tag explicitly with `git push origin v1.0`.

---

## Step 8: Deleting `testbranch` Locally and on the Remote

Since `testbranch` had been fully merged into `main` and the project was tagged, the branch was no longer needed. Keeping old branches around can make the repository confusing over time, especially in large collaborative projects, so I deleted it from both my local machine and from GitHub.

```bash
# Delete testbranch locally
# -d is the safe flag: only deletes if the branch has been merged
git branch -d testbranch

# Delete testbranch from GitHub
git push origin --delete testbranch
```

I used `git branch -d` (lowercase d) rather than `-D` because the lowercase version checks that the branch has been merged before deleting. If I had tried to delete an unmerged branch, git would have warned me — this protects against accidentally deleting work that has not yet been integrated.

