## Step 9: Viewing the Commit Log in Condensed Form

To get a clear overview of everything I had done throughout this project, I ran the following command in the terminal:

```bash
git log --oneline --graph --all
```

This produced output similar to:

```
* a3f1c2d (HEAD -> main, tag: v1.0, origin/main) Resolve merge conflict: combine dataset descriptions
* 8b2e4a1 Modify example.qmd on main: update dataset description  
* 5d9c7f3 Add dataset introduction section to example.qmd on testbranch
* 1a4b8e2 Initial commit: add project files and example.qmd
```

`--oneline` shows each commit on a single line with its short hash and message, which makes it easy to scan. `--graph` draws a simple text diagram showing where branches diverged and merged. `--all` includes commits from every branch, not just the current one. Together, these flags give a clear and compact picture of the entire project history.

