## Step 10: Adding a Plot, Committing, and Undoing the Commit

For the final step, I added a new section to `example.qmd` containing a simple plot:

```{r}
# A simple scatter plot to demonstrate adding new content
plot(pressure,
     main = "Vapour Pressure of Mercury vs Temperature",
     xlab = "Temperature (°C)",
     ylab = "Pressure (mm Hg)",
     col = "steelblue",
     pch = 16)
```

I then staged and committed the change as usual:

```bash
git add example.qmd
git commit -m "Add pressure plot section to example.qmd"
```

Then I practised undoing this commit **without losing** the changes I had made to the file:

```bash
# Undo the last commit, but keep the file changes in the working directory
git reset HEAD~1
```

`git reset HEAD~1` moves the branch pointer back by one commit, which removes the commit from the history. Because I did not use the `--hard` flag, the default behaviour (`--mixed`) leaves my file changes intact in the working directory — the plot code is still in `example.qmd`, it is just no longer part of any commit. This is useful when I want to revise a commit before pushing, or when I accidentally committed something I was not ready to save yet.

I confirmed it worked by running:

```bash
git log --oneline   # The plot commit is gone from history
git status          # example.qmd shows as modified but unstaged
```

Both outputs confirmed that the commit had been removed but the file content was preserved — exactly what `git reset HEAD~1` is supposed to do.

