## Summary

Throughout this guide, I have demonstrated the following git skills and the reasoning behind each one:

- **`git init` + `git remote add`** — setting up version control and connecting to GitHub
- **`git add` + `git commit`** — staging and saving snapshots of work with clear messages
- **`git checkout -b`** — creating branches to isolate changes safely
- **`git commit --amend`** — rewriting the last commit to keep history clean
- **`git merge` + conflict resolution** — combining branches and manually fixing conflicts
- **`git tag -a`** — marking important milestones with annotated tags
- **`git branch -d` + `git push --delete`** — cleaning up merged branches safely
- **`git log --oneline --graph --all`** — reading a compact view of project history
- **`git reset HEAD~1`** — undoing a commit while preserving local changes

Each of these tools has a specific purpose, and understanding *why* to use each one — not just *how* — is what allows me to collaborate effectively and maintain a clean, understandable project history.
